		{
			"id": "112688785021922871",
			"parentId": "112687668550361735",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/112688785021922871",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-06-27T13:20:00.358Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://flaki.social/@flaki\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>flaki</span></a></span> “the JS ecosystem likes to pretend every script still runs in Netscape Navigator 4.0” that doesn’t resonate with my experience—do you think this is a widespread thing or just this one person?</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112688826090003834",
			"parentId": "112688785021922871",
			"url": "https://flaki.social/@flaki/112688826061598595",
			"author": {
				"name": "Flaki",
				"url": "https://flaki.social/@flaki",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/315/100/329/990/772/original/74ebe12d2f7e951a.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-06-27T13:30:26.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I'm sure part of the (source of the) problem is the \"I cannot possibly know of and/or cater for every runtime and every usecase\" which is a hard ~~computer~~ social problem to solve, particularly for OSS \"provided as is and without fitness for any purpose\" etc., and while above I've certainly been exaggerating somewhat, I more meant it in that JS still can't or doesn't want to recognize just how widespread/abundant it is, and while individual devs can argue that they shouldn't be required to support every use case (the certainly needn't!), centrals gatekeepers such as npm has been excruciatingly slow to react to this diversification...</p><p>(part of the reason, of course, is *having* central gatekeepers like npm in the first place, but while I really like e.g. Deno's approach where Ryan &amp; co tried to fix this particular mistake, it's a mistake we're likely stuck with for good...)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112688862493903079",
			"parentId": "112688826090003834",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/112688862493903079",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-06-27T13:39:42.487Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://flaki.social/@flaki\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>flaki</span></a></span> ah, yeah—that makes more sense. Certainly “JS still can&#39;t or doesn&#39;t want to recognize just how widespread/abundant it is.”</p><p>I don’t think I agree that centralization is the root problem though. I don’t see Deno fixing this either. I see something like Google’s Baseline as a path forward here—each package needs to declare it’s intended use and compatibility.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112688917799215335",
			"parentId": "112688862493903079",
			"url": "https://flaki.social/@flaki/112688917769224772",
